# Line endings.
#
# The repository stores text as LF. Without this file the answer depends
# on each developer's local `core.autocrlf`, which is a per-machine
# setting that does not travel with a clone — so one Windows checkout can
# turn every tracked file into a 60,000-line whitespace diff and nobody
# can see a real change in `git status` any more.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text by inspecting content, and
# `eol=lf` fixes what it writes into the repository. Working trees still
# get whatever the platform wants on checkout.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries: never touched, never diffed as text. Without these a stray
# byte sequence can make git guess "text" and corrupt the file on
# checkout.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.pdf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.eot    binary
*.zip    binary
*.db     binary
*.sqlite binary
*.drawio binary
*.xlsx   binary
*.docx   binary

# Windows shells genuinely need CRLF: cmd.exe mis-parses a LF-only batch
# file that ends mid-token. None exist today; the rule is here so adding
# one later does not need this file revisited.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.ps1    text eol=crlf
